{{quotation|Now Ama-terasu no Oho-kami instructed Yamato-hime no Mikoto, saying:—"The province of Ise, of the divine wind, is the land whither repair the waves from the eternal world, the successive waves. It is a secluded and pleasant land. In this land I wish to dwell." In compliance, therefore, with the instruction of the Great Goddess, a shrine was erected to her in the province of Ise. Accordingly an [[Abstinence Palace]] was built at Kaha-kami in Isuzu. This was called the palace of Iso. It was there that Ama-terasu no Oho-kami first descended from Heaven.<ref>{{cite wikisource |author-first= William George |author-last= Aston |chapter= Book I |wslink= Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697 |plaintitle= Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697 |year= 1896 |publisher= Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner & Co.|wspages=176|draft}}</ref>|draft}}
This account serves as the origin myth of the [[Ise Grand Shrine|Grand Shrine of Ise]], Amaterasu's chief place of worship.|draft}} Sujin placed his daughter {{Nihongo|{{Ill|Toyosukiiri-hime|ja|豊鍬入姫命|draft}}|豊鍬入姫命|draft}} in charge of the new shrine, and she would become the first [[Ise Saiō]],<ref>{{cite web | url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=8608 | title=Encyclopedia of Shinto詳細 }}</ref>entrusting with her the mirror and sword, she brought them to the village of Kasanuhi.<ref name="aston-1896a" /><ref name="meiji-japan-society-1925a" /> {{Ill|Yamato Okunitama|lt=Yamato-no-Okunitama|ja|倭大国魂神|simple|draft}} (the other god) was entrusted to another daughter named {{Ill|Nunaki-iri-hime|ja|渟名城入姫命|draft}}, but her health began to fail shortly afterward. It is recorded that Nunakiiri-hime became [[emaciated]] after losing all of her hair, which rendered her unable to perform her duties.<ref name="aston150–164" /> These events still did not alleviate the ongoing plague sweeping the empire, so Sujin decreed a [[divination]] to be performed sometime during the 7th year of his reign. The divination involved him making a trip to the plain of Kami-asaji or {{Nihongo|Kamu-asaji-ga-hara|神浅茅原|draft}}, and invoking the {{ill|Yaoyorozu no Kami|lt=eighty myriad deities|simple||draft}}.<ref name="aston150–164" /> This worship of the deities is seen as being potentially linked to defining a more complex social order, and organizing the deities of many clans across the region.<ref>{{Citation |title=Contents |date=2017-12-31 |url=http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/9780824862848-001 |work=Himiko and Japan's Elusive Chiefdom of Yamatai |pages=189–191 |access-date=2023-10-24 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |doi=10.1515/9780824862848-001 |isbn=978-0-8248-6284-8|draft}}</ref>
