Sujin's aunt {{Nihongo|{{ill|Princess_Yamato_Totohi_Momoso|lt=Yamatototohimomoso-hime|ja|倭迹迹日百襲姫命}}|倭迹迹日百襲媛命|}} (daughter of 7th Emperor {{ill|Emperor Kōrei|en}}) acted as a ''{{ill|miko|en}}'', and was possessed by a god who identified himself as {{ill|Ōmononushi|en}},<ref name="aston150–164" /><ref name="ellwood-1990a">{{Cite journal |last=Ellwood |first=Robert S. |date=1990 |title=The Sujin Religious Revolution |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/30234018 |journal=Japanese Journal of Religious Studies |volume=17 |issue=2/3 |pages=199–217 |doi=10.18874/jjrs.17.2-3.1990.199-217 |jstor=30234018 |issn=0304-1042}}</ref> possibly the same entity as {{Ill|Yamato Okunitama|lt=Yamato-no-Okunitama|ja|倭大国魂神|simple}}.  {{ill|Ichishi no Nagaochi|ja|市磯長尾市}} would conduct the Okunitama rites replacing the emaciated {{Ill|Nunaki-iri-hime|ja|渟名城入姫命}}.<ref name="ellwood-1990a" /> {{ill|Ichishi no Nagaochi|ja|市磯長尾市}} would be the ancestor of the {{Interlanguage link|Yamato no Kuni no Miyatsuko|lt=Yamato clan|ja|倭国造|simple}}.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Page:Nihongi by Aston.djvu/208 - Wikisource, the free online library |url=https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Page:Nihongi_by_Aston.djvu/208 |access-date=2023-10-24 |website=en.wikisource.org |language=en}}</ref> This replacement is taken as a shift towards more patriarchai religion.<ref name="ellwood-1990a" /> This god claimed responsibility for the plague, announcing that it would not stop until he was venerated. Although the Emperor propitiated to the god, the effects were not immediate. Sujin was later given guidance in the form of a dream to seek out a man named {{Nihongo|{{ill|Ōtataneko|ja|大田田根子命}}|太田田根子}} and appoint him as head priest.<ref name="aston150–164" /> He eventually found him in {{ill|Izumo Province|en}}.<ref name="ellwood-1990a" /> When he was found and installed, the pestilence eventually subsided, allowing five cereal crops to ripen.<ref name="aston150–164" /> Out of an abundance of caution, the Emperor also appointed {{Nihongo|Ikagashikoo|伊香色雄}} as {{Nihongo|''kami-no-mono-akatsu-hito''|神班物者}}, or one who sorts the offerings to the gods.<ref name="chamberlain-basil-1882a">{{cite book |author=Chamberlain, Basil |url=http://www.sacred-texts.com/shi/kj/kj072.htm |title=''[SECT. LXV.—EMPEROR SŪ-JIN (PART III: STORY OF OHO-TATA-NE-KO'S BIRTH)]'' (''The Kojiki'') |publisher=Read before the Asiatic Society of Japan on April 12, May 10, and June 21, 1882, reprinted in 1919 |page=219 |quote=His Augustness Oho-tata-ne-ko&nbsp;... was the ancestor of the Dukes of Miwa and of the Dukes of Kamo. |author-link=Basil Chamberlain}}</ref> To this day the {{ill|Miwa clan|lt=Miwa sept|en}} of the {{ill|Kamo clan|en}} claim to be descents from {{ill|Ōtataneko|ja|大田田根子命}}, while Ikagashikoo was a claimed ancestor of the now extinct {{ill|Mononobe clan|en}}.<ref name="chamberlain-basil-1882a" /> This has been suggested as representing a population migration from {{ill|Izumo Province|Izumo|en}}.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Kidder |first=J. Edward |url=http://dx.doi.org/10.1515/9780824862848 |title=Himiko and Japan's Elusive Chiefdom of Yamatai |date=2017-12-18 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-6284-8 |pages=152–168|doi=10.1515/9780824862848 }}</ref>
