Sujin's aunt {{Nihongo|{{ill|Princess_Yamato_Totohi_Momoso|lt=Yamatototohimomoso-hime|ja|倭迹迹日百襲姫命|en}}|倭迹迹日百襲媛命|}} (daughter of 7th Emperor {{ill|Emperor Kōrei|en}}) acted as a ''{{ill|miko|en}}'', and was possessed by a god who identified himself as {{ill|Ōmononushi|en}},<ref name="aston150–164" /><ref name="ellwood-1990a">{{Cite journal |last=Ellwood |first=Robert S. |date=1990 |title=The Sujin Religious Revolution |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/30234018 |journal=Japanese Journal of Religious Studies |volume=17 |issue=2/3 |pages=199–217 |doi=10.18874/jjrs.17.2-3.1990.199-217 |jstor=30234018 |issn=0304-1042}}</ref> possibly the same entity as {{Ill|Yamato Okunitama|lt=Yamato-no-Okunitama|ja|倭大国魂神|simple}}.  {{ill|Ichishi no Nagaochi|ja|市磯長尾市|en:draft|6=simple|7=User:Immanuelle/Ichishi no Nagaochi}} would conduct the Okunitama rites replacing the emaciated {{Ill|Nunaki-iri-hime|ja|渟名城入姫命|en:draft|6=simple|7=User:Immanuelle/Nunaki-iri-hime}}.<ref name="ellwood-1990a" /> {{ill|Ichishi no Nagaochi|ja|市磯長尾市|en:draft|6=simple|7=User:Immanuelle/Ichishi no Nagaochi}} would be the ancestor of the {{Interlanguage link|Yamato no Kuni no Miyatsuko|lt=Yamato clan|ja|倭国造|simple}}.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Page:Nihongi by Aston.djvu/208 - Wikisource, the free online library |url=https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Page:Nihongi_by_Aston.djvu/208 |access-date=2023-10-24 |website=en.wikisource.org |language=en}}</ref> This replacement is taken as a shift towards more patriarchai religion.<ref name="ellwood-1990a" /> This god claimed responsibility for the plague, announcing that it would not stop until he was venerated. Although the Emperor propitiated to the god, the effects were not immediate. Sujin was later given guidance in the form of a dream to seek out a man named {{Nihongo|{{ill|Ōtataneko|ja|大田田根子命}}|太田田根子}} and appoint him as head priest.<ref name="aston150–164" /> He eventually found him in {{ill|Izumo Province|en}}.<ref name="ellwood-1990a" /> When he was found and installed,